<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=telephone">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telephone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The telephone is a device that turns the voice into electrical signals and back into sound at a distance; Johann Philipp Reis demonstrated the first device to transmit a voice by electronic signals before the Physical Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 26 October 1861. Credit for the invention is disputed among Reis, Antonio Meucci, Charles Bourseul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a> Gray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a>, who in March 1876 received the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> patent for the electric telephone. Bell's patent became the master patent from which others flowed, and switchboards soon linked telephones into exchanges and networks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telephone.jpg" alt="Telephone" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1896_telephone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1861</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Frankfurt</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Johann Philipp Reis · Alexander Graham Bell</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Credit is disputed among Reis, Antonio Meucci, Charles Bourseul, Elisha Gray and Alexander Graham Bell, who received the first United States patent in 1876.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Electrical telegraph<span>1816</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mobile-phone">Mobile phone<span>1973</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=telephone">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
